.TH LOCKERD 1 lockerd\-@VERSION@
.SH NAME
lockerd \- screen locking daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lockerd
.RB [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.P
Daemon that sets up screen locking and screen saver.
.br
Mainly a wrapper around xss-lock(1), lockerd is the centerpiece that builds the
environment for the betterxsecurelock setup that handles integrations to create
a modern and good looking screen locking and screen saver setup that uses
xsecurelock and xss-lock, the xss-lock program handles hooking up the screen
locker to the XServer X Screen Saver events as well as Login Manager events such
as systemd or elogind, lockerd handles the configuration and behaviours around,
such as setting up the X Screen Saver timeout, the idle time to lock and the
time to off the screen as well as monitoring for discrepancies between the
configured timeut settings with the running time settings and correcting them if
needed.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B "\-h, help"
Print brief usage information to standard output and exit.

.TP
.B "\-d, debug"
Show debug output.

.TP
.B "\-c, config  [ CONFIG ]"
Pass lockerd a CONFIG file to use.
.br
The default config file is at $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/better-xsecurelock/config

.TP
.B "\-l, logfile  [ LOGFILE ]"
Pass lockerd a LOGFILE file to use.
.br
The default logfile is at $XDG_STATE_HOME/better-xsecurelock/lockerd

.TP
.B "\-s, session-id  [ XDG_SESSION_ID ]"
Pass lockerd the XDG_SESSION_ID if it cannot be passed by environment variable.

.SH SIGNALS
.P
In addition the daemon also reacts to the following signals:
.TP
.B "HUP"
Reload the config.

.TP
.B "USR1"
Reload the config.

.TP
.B "USR2"
Relaunch the program.

.SH AUTHOR
.EX
eylles                 <ed.ylles1997 at gmail.com>
.EE
.SH HOMEPAGE
.TP
